--- body ---
## Context

Tickets 0551-0556 (and 0565, filed alongside this one) each build and test
one module of the conductor in isolation, against fixtures and virtual
time: the ledger/tick, election/lease, extract shim, the v2 schema, and
(once 0565 lands) the segmentation write path. None of them assembles a
real, running process. SPEC.md §5.2.5 describes the actual topology: N×P0
processes, one elected conductor, one run-to-drain pipeline worker
communicating over a full-duplex stdio pipe, the conductor opening
`search-index-v2.sqlite` for real and running the tick on a real cadence.

This ticket is the assembly point, and the author's stated acceptance bar
for it: point the assembled process at a fixture library (tranche 1's
synthetic ~50-item library, served through its record/replay fake of the
local API) and get a real, queryable `search-index-v2.sqlite` out the
other end — entries, slabs, passages, and vectors populated by an actual
run of the pipeline, not by unit tests calling internal methods directly.

This is explicitly short of "the real thing" (the author's actual Zotero
library, replacing or sitting beside v1 in production). Two further gaps
stay out of this ticket's scope, flagged for separate decisions: wiring
`search-index-v2.sqlite` into the actual MCP query path (0556 marked this
out of its own scope too), and any migration/coexistence story for a user
who already has a v1 index. Both need the author's call on shape before
they're ticket-sized.

## Relevant files

- `SPEC.md` §5.2.5 — full process topology: the lease-elected conductor,
  the run-to-drain worker, the full-duplex pipe ("the conductor drains
  arriving windows and records before blocking on any write of work
  orders, in both directions"), orphan repair (stdin EOF on the worker
  side, lease-loss kill on the conductor side).
- Fork `src/index.ts` — v1's current entry point, for the pattern this
  ticket's new path should follow structurally; this ticket ADDS a
  conductor-aware path, it does not replace or modify v1's, per the
  parallel-schema directive that shaped 0556.
- Everything tranches 1-3, 0556, and 0565 built: `ledger.ts`, `lease.ts`,
  `election.ts`, `conductor.ts`, `orphan.ts`, the extract-stage modules,
  0556's schema additions, 0565's segmentation loop. Read what actually
  landed — this ticket is the integration point, so its accuracy depends
  on reading the real merged code, not this description.
- Tranche 1's fixtures (`tests/fixtures/local-api-replay.ts`,
  `synthetic-library.ts`) — the fixture library and the fake local API
  this ticket's acceptance test points the assembled process at.

## Actions

1. A real process entry point that opens `search-index-v2.sqlite` (the
   file 0556 wires) alongside — never instead of — v1's
   `search-index.sqlite`.
2. Wire election (tranche 2) so genuinely separate P0 instances (real OS
   processes, not simulated connections in one test process — tranche 2's
   own soak test used separate SQLite connections, not separate
   processes; this ticket is where that gap closes) correctly elect one
   conductor.
3. Spawn the pipeline worker as a real child process on ledger-queue
   non-empty, run-to-drain, exit at empty — per SPEC's topology, not a
   thread or an in-process simulation.
4. Wire the full-duplex stdio pipe between conductor and worker exactly
   as SPEC describes it (windows and records flowing one way, work orders
   the other, neither side blocking the other's drain).
5. Make the local-API source swappable: a real Zotero local API for
   production use, tranche 1's record/replay fixture for a dry run
   against the fixture library — same conductor code, different source.
6. Connect tranche 2's orphan-repair primitives (`orphan.ts`) to the real
   process lifecycles established here — they exist and are tested in
   isolation; this is where they're actually called.

## Test

- The acceptance test: point the assembled process at tranche 1's
  fixture library through the replay fake, run it to completion (or to a
  stable idle state under virtual/accelerated time), and assert
  `search-index-v2.sqlite` ends up populated — entries, slabs, passages,
  vectors — and directly queryable (raw SQL against the file is enough
  here; wiring the MCP query path is a separate, later ticket).
- A multi-process election test: launch genuinely separate OS processes
  (not simulated connections) against one data directory, kill -9 the
  conductor, confirm another takes over within TTL + cadence — the same
  property tranche 2 tested, now over real processes.

## Verification

- [ ] Election works across genuinely separate OS processes
- [ ] The pipe survives a worker crash and a conductor handover without
      corrupting in-flight work
- [ ] A full run against the fixture library completes and the resulting
      `search-index-v2.sqlite` is queryable by direct inspection
- [ ] Orphan repair fires in both directions under a real process kill,
      not only in the unit-level simulation tranche 2 shipped

## Invariants

- v1's `search-index.sqlite` and its query path are untouched — this is
  an additional capability, not a replacement.
- Upstream tests green throughout.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] A runnable process assembly merged on a fork branch
- [ ] An end-to-end index build against the fixture library succeeds and
      is verified queryable
- [x] The open question in this ticket's log (whether this needed to wait
      on 0555) is resolved and recorded, whichever way it went — settled
      2026-09-01: no wait, the acceptance assertions are order-insensitive
